+The `settings.*`, `credentials.*`, and `llm.*` domains are the configuration-page wire. The settings domain serves the namespaces addressed by registered configurable providers (`ctx.llm.listConfigurableProviders()`) plus a small explicit allowlist — the Web preference `permission` and the product-owned `ui-onboarding`. When the [web-config-crawler](../../../packages/host/web-config-crawler/README.md) plugin is mounted, the domain additionally serves EVERY registered settings namespace — the deployment's explicit opt-in to a fully automatic configuration surface, with no per-plugin action needed. Without the crawler, adding a Settings registration alone never makes it remotely readable or writable. Any other namespace answers `settings-not-exposed` — the same answer an unregistered namespace gets, so no caller can enumerate the registry by probing. The `composition.*` domain rides the same crawler: `composition.describe` lists every mounted plugin whose cordis.yml row carries a `Config` schema (redacted), `composition.update` applies path-addressed ops host-side to the plugin's current resolved configuration and persists the full row into the personal overlay, and `composition.remove` deletes the overlay row so the next boot reverts to the lower layers. Without the crawler the domain answers an actionable `internal` error; write refusals map to `composition-rejected {id}`. `settings.describe` returns each exposed namespace's serialized schemastery schema, redacted layered values (resolved/`base`/`user` — a field's presence in `user` marks it user-overridden), the `secrets` slot list, and the section's `revision`. `settings.update`/`settings.replace` write the user layer; `settings.mutate` applies path ops (`set`/`unset`) against the section as stored, which is the removal path for a client holding the redacted view — rebuilding a section from it and replacing wholesale would delete the secrets the wire never returned. Any write may carry `expectedRevision`; a stale